To scale this into a multi-camera system, I would keep the current microservice pipeline but make identity management cross-camera instead of camera-local.

Core idea
Right now the system works well for single-camera surveillance:

detect
track
event detection
plate recognition
graph storage
For multi-camera scaling, I would extend it in 3 layers:

1. Camera-wise horizontal scaling
Run one ingestion/tracking pipeline per camera or camera shard.
That means:

each camera publishes its own detection stream
tracking remains local to that camera
services can scale independently using workers and stream partitions
This is the easiest first scale step.

2. Cross-camera identity layer
Track IDs are only local to one camera, so for multi-camera systems I would introduce a global identity layer using:

plate number when available
time proximity
camera topology
vehicle appearance embeddings if needed
That gives:

local track ID
global vehicle identity
So the same vehicle seen in cam_1 and cam_5 can be linked even though track IDs differ.

3. Knowledge graph as the unifying layer
Neo4j becomes more valuable in multi-camera scale.
I would store:

Vehicle
Camera
Sighting
optionally Transition between cameras
Then we can answer:

where was this vehicle seen?
in what sequence?
how often does it reappear?
what is the likely path across cameras?
What else I would change for scale

move Redis streams toward per-camera or partitioned streams
add worker scaling and consumer groups per service
separate hot path from heavy analytics
use object storage for frames instead of passing too much image data inline if throughput increases a lot
add retry queues and dead-letter handling
use UTC/GMT consistently for all timestamps
add monitoring for lag, dropped frames, API rate limits, and service health
Main challenge in multi-camera
The hardest problem is not just detection.
It is cross-camera re-identification:

same vehicle
different angle
different lighting
different camera
possible missing plate
So in production I would combine:

ANPR
temporal constraints
camera location graph
optional appearance-based vehicle re-ID
Short interview version
To scale it to multi-camera, I would keep tracking local to each camera, introduce a global vehicle identity layer across cameras using ANPR plus temporal and appearance cues, partition streams and workers by camera, and use Neo4j to model vehicle movement and sightings across the camera network.